WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Scenario: A risk register lists a low-likelihood event that

2
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.720
could expose a large customer database. Existing controls are

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.080
documented, but their current effectiveness has not been tested.

4
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.440
Safest response: Option B: Assess likelihood and impact, validate

5
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.547
existing control effectiveness, estimate residual risk and record assumptions and uncertainty.

6
00:00:17.547 --> 00:00:20.907
Risk assessment should consider both likelihood and consequence, then

7
00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:24.267
evaluate how controls change residual exposure. Option A is

8
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:27.627
unsafe because Low likelihood alone ignores potentially severe impact

9
00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.987
and uncertainty about control effectiveness. Option C is unsafe

10
00:00:30.987 --> 00:00:34.347
because Automatic maximum ratings do not provide a consistent

11
00:00:34.347 --> 00:00:37.707
or useful risk prioritization method. Option D is unsafe

12
00:00:37.707 --> 00:00:41.067
because Documented controls are not necessarily effective; the risk

13
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:44.427
remains until assessed and treated appropriately. In practice: Validate

14
00:00:44.427 --> 00:00:47.787
control operation, document evidence and uncertainty, calculate or qualitatively

15
00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:51.147
rate residual risk, then assign treatment and ownership. Key

16
00:00:51.147 --> 00:00:54.507
takeaway: Risk decisions require likelihood, impact, control evidence, residual

17
00:00:54.507 --> 00:00:56.000
risk and explicit assumptions.
